\par }\pard\plain \s33\qc\widctlpar \f5\fs20\lang2057 }}\pard\plain \s33\qc\widctlpar \f5\fs20\lang2057 {\caps\fs24 
\par \page }{\fs24 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\fi-432\li432\sb480\widctlpar\tx432\tx794\tx2127\tx2410\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186350}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104070}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269665}1\tab Introduction{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186350}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104070}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269665}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 This document defines the procedure for evaluating the performance of objective video quality models submitted to the Video Quality Expert Group (VQEG{\cs34\fs16\up6 \chftn 
{\footnote \pard\plain \s35\fi-256\li256\sb136\keep\widctlpar\tx256\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs20\lang2057 {\cs34\fs22\up6 \chftn }{\fs22  Contact: Arthur Webster, +1 303-4973567, E-mail webster@its.bldrdoc.gov}}}
) formed from experts of ITU-T Study Groups 9 and 12 and ITU-R Study Group 11. It is based on discussions at the 1{\super st}
  Meeting of VQEG, October 14-16, 1997, Turin, Italy. This test plan was offered to the participating ITU Study Groups (ITU-T Study Groups 9 and 12 and ITU-R Study Group 11) for further review and comment. It was further modified during the 2{\super nd}
  VQEG meeting, May 27-29,1998, Gaithersburg, USA.
\par The objective models will be tested using a set of test sequences selected by the VQEG Independents Labs and Selection Committee (ILSC). The test sequences will be processed through a number of hypothetical reference conditions (HRC\rquote 
s) to be finalized by the ILSC and based on the list of HRC\rquote s proposed at the May 27-29,1998, Gaithersburg, USA.
\par The quality predictions of the models will be compared with subjective ratings by the viewers of the test sequences as defined by the VQEG Subjective Test Plan. The Subjective Test Plan has two separate but overlapping subjective test experiments to cover
 the intende
d bit rate range of 768 kbit/s to 50 Mbit/s, and the model performance will be compared separately with the results from each of the two subjective test experiments. Based on the VQEG evaluation of proposed models, the goal is to recommend method(s) for o
bjective measurement of digital video quality for bit rates ranging from 768 kbit/s to 50 Mbit/s. The preference is one recommended model, but multiple models are possible. The final list of HRC\rquote s is given in the subjective testplan.
\par \page 
\par \pard\plain \s1\fi-432\li432\sb480\widctlpar\tx432\tx794\tx2127\tx2410\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186351}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104071}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269666}2\tab Data formats and processing{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186351}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104071}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269666}
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186352}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104072}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269667}2.1\tab Video data format, general{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186352}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104072}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269667}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 
Objective models will take two Rec. 601 digital video sequences as input, referred to as Source and Processed, with the goal of predicting the quality difference between the Source and Processed sequences. The video sequences will be in either 625/50 or 5
25/60 format. The choice of HRC\rquote s and Processing will assure that the following operations {\ul do not} occur between Source and Processed sequence pairs:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}\pard \fi-360\li360\sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}Visible picture cropping
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}Chroma/luma differential timing
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}Picture jitter
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}Spatial scaling (size change)
\par \pard \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104073}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269668}2.2\tab Model input and output data format{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104073}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269668}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 The models will be given two ASCII lists of sequences to be processed, one for 525/60 and one for 625/50. {\lang1033 
These input files are ASCII files, listing pairs of video sequence files to be processed.  Each line of this file has the following format:
\par \tab <source-file>\tab  <processed-file>
\par where <source-file> is the name of a source video sequence file and <processed-file> is the name of a processed video sequence file, whose format 
is specified in section 2.5 of this document. File names may include a path. Source and processed video sequence files must contain the exact sequence pattern specified in section 2.3 and section 2.5. For example, an input file for the 525/60 case might c
ontain the following:
\par }{\b\lang1033 /video/src1_525.yuv     /video/src1_hrc2_525.yuv
\par /video/src1_525.yuv     /video/src1_hrc1_525.yuv
\par /video/src2_525.yuv     /video/src2_hrc1_525.yuv
\par /video/src2_525.yuv     /video/src2_hrc2_525.yuv
\par }From these lists the models are allowed to generate their model setting files from which the model can be ran.
\par {\lang1033 
The output file is an ASCII file created by the model program, listing the name of processed sequence and the resulting Video Quality Rating (VQR) of the model. The contents of the output file should be flushed after each sequence is processed, to allow t
he testing labs the option of halting a processing run at any time. Alternately the models may create an individual output file for each setting file and collect all data into a single 
output file using a separate collect program. Each line of the ASCII output  file has the following format:
\par \tab <processed-file>  VQR
\par Where <processed-file> is the name of the processed sequence run through this model, without any path information; and VQR is the Video Quality Rating produced by the objective model. For the input file example, this file contains the following:
\par 
\par }{\b\lang1033 src1_hrc2_525.yuv  0.150
\par src1_hrc1_525.yuv  1.304
\par src2_hrc1_525.yuv  0.102
\par src2_hrc2_525.yuv  2.989
\par }{\lang1033 
\par Each proponent is also allowed to output a file containing Model Output Values (MOV\rquote s) which the proponents consider to be important. The format of this file will be
\par }{\b\lang1033 src1_hrc2_525.yuv  0.150  }{\b MOV}{\b\sub 1}{\b   MOV}{\b\sub 2}{\b ,\'85   MOV}{\b\sub N}{\b\lang1033 
\par src1_hrc1_525.yuv  1.304 }{\b  MOV}{\b\sub 1}{\b   MOV}{\b\sub 2}{\b ,\'85   MOV}{\b\sub N}{\b\lang1033 
\par src2_hrc1_525.yuv  0.102}{\b   MOV}{\b\sub 1}{\b   MOV}{\b\sub 2}{\b ,\'85   MOV}{\b\sub N}{\b\lang1033 
\par src2_hrc2_525.yuv  2.989}{\b   MOV}{\b\sub 1}{\b   MOV}{\b\sub 2}{\b ,\'85   MOV}{\b\sub N}{\b\lang1033 
\par }
\par {\lang1033 All video sequences will be displayed in overscan and the non-active video region is defined as:
\par the top 14 frame lines 
\par the bottom 14 frame lines 
\par the left 14 pixels
\par the right 14 pixels.
\par Possib
le small differences between individual monitors are averaged out in the analysis of the subjective data. A sanity check for large deviations from the above non-active region will be carried out by the subjective test labs. If in the normalization a diffe
rent active region is found and the cropping size is such that it will be visible within the active video region this sequence will not be used. }
\par \pard \sb136\widctlpar\tx0\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 Models will only get one input parameter, the 525/60 versus 625/50 input format, in the form of two separate lists. All other parameters like screen distance (5H), maximum luminance level (70 cd/m{
\fs15\up11 2}), background luminance,  video format, gamma of the monitor, etc are fixed for the test and thus are not required for the setting files.
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\b0 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186353}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104074}
\par }{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269669}2.3\tab Test sequence normalization{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186353}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104074}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269669}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 {\lang1033 
As a Source video sequence passes through an HRC, it is possible that the resulting Processed sequence has a number of scaling and alignment differences from the Source sequence. To facilitate a common analysis of various objective quality measurement met
hods (referred to as models), Tektronix will normalize the Processed sequences to remove the following deterministic differences that may have been introduced by a typical HRC:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}Global temporal frame shift (aligned to {{\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 177 \\f 
"Symbol" \\s 11}{\fldrslt\f1\fs22}}}0 field error)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}Global horizontal/vertical spatial image shift (aligned to {{\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 177 \\f "Symbol" \\s 11}{\fldrslt\f1\fs22}}}0.1 pixel)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}Global chroma/luma gain and offset ( accuracy to be defined)
\par \pard \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\pndec }
The normalized sequences will be used for both subjective and objective ratings. The normalized sequences will be sent on D-1 digital video tape to the Subjective Testing Labs for the DSCQS (Double Stimulus Continuous Quality Scale) rating. The normalized
 sequences will also be used for analysis by the objective models. The sequences will be available on computer tape for the objective ratings in the following two formats:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\i\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}\pard \fi-360\li360\sb136\widctlpar\tx360\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
8 mm Exabyte format (archived in UNIX tar format with a block factor of 1){\b\i 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\b\i\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}}4 mm DDS3 format (details to be defined) {\b\i 
\par }\pard \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 
The first and last second of the sequences will contain  an alignment pattern to facilitate the normalization operation. The pattern is a coded set of alternating light/dark blocks in the upper half of the image (provided by Tektronix) and will not be inc
luded in the portion of the sequence shown to subjective assessors. The required normalization will be estimated with a non-confidential set of algorithms (provided by Tektronix) over the first second alignment pattern p
ortion of the sequence. The normalization from the first second estimate will then be applied uniformly over the length of the sequence on the assumption that the differences needing normalization are invariant over the sequence length. The last second of
 alignment pattern may be used to determine if the values have remained constant through the length of the sequence. Finally ten frames before the 8 seconds video sequence and ten frames after the 8 seconds video sequence will not be used in both the obje
ctive and subjective evaluation. A complete sequence {\lang1033 on D-1 tape and Exabyte/DAT will be}:
\par {\f29\lang1033 AlignmentPattern(1sec)+ VideoNotUsed(10frames)+ }{\b\f29\lang1033 Video(8sec)}{\f29\lang1033 +VideoNotUsed(10 frames)+AlignmentPattern(1 sec)}
\par The normalization will be done by Tektronix between August 7{\super th}, 1998 and September 11{\super th} when all proponents have submitted and run their models.
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186354}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104075}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269670}2.4\tab Test sequence objective analysis{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186354}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104075}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269670} 
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 Each proponent receives normalized Source and Processed video sequences after September 25{\super th}, 1998. Each proponent analyzes all the 
video sequences and sends the results to the Independent Labs and Selection Committee (ILSC) before December 11{\super th}, 1998.
\par The independent lab(s) must have running in their lab the software that was provided by the proponents, see section {\b\i 3.2}
. To reduce the work load on the independent lab(s), the independent lab(s) will verify a random sequence subset (about 20%) of all video sequences to verify that the software produces the same results as the proponents within an acceptable error of 0.1%.
 The random 30 se
quence subset will be selected by the ILSC and kept confidential to the ILSC. If errors greater than 0.1% are found, then the independent lab and proponent lab will work together to analyze intermediate results and attempt to discover sources of errors. I
f processing and handling  errors are ruled out, then the ILSC will review the final and intermediate results and recommend further action.
\par The model output will be a single Video Quality Rating (VQR) number calculated over the sequence length (or a subset)
 not containing the alignment patterns. The VQR is expected to correlate with the Difference between the Source and Processed Mean Opinion Scores (MOS) resulting from the VQEG\rquote s subjective testing experiment. This Difference in subjective MOS
\rquote s is referred to as DMOS. It is expected that the VQR\rquote s and DMOS\rquote 
s will be positive in typical situations and increasing values will predict increasingly perceptible differences between Source and Processed sequences. Negative values of both may occur in certain situations and will be allowed.
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186355}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104076}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269671}2.5\tab Data format, specifics{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186355}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104076}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269671}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 
The test video sequences will be in ITU Recommendation 601 4:2:2 component video format using an aspect ratio of 4:3. This may be in either 525/60 or 625/50 line formats. The temporal ordering of fields F1 and F2 will be described below with the field con
taining line 1 of (stored) video referred to as the Top-Field.
\par {\i\fs24\ul Data storage:
\par }A LINE: of video consists of 1440 8 bit data fields in the multiplexed order: Cb Y Cr [Y] ... . Hence there are 720 Y's and 360 Cb's and 360 Cr's per line of video.
\par 
\par A FRAME: of video consists of 486 active lines for 525/60 Hz material and 576 active lines for 625/50 Hz material. Each frame consists of two interlaced Fields, F1 and F2. The temporal ordering of F1 and F2 can be easily confused due to cropping and so we
 make it specific as follows:
\par For 525/60 material: F1--the Top-Field-- (containing line 1 of FILE storage) is temporally LATER (than field F2). F1 and F2 are stored interlaced.
\par For 625/50 material: F1--the Top-Field-- is temporally EARLIER than F2.
\par The Frame SIZE:
\par  for 525/60 is: 699840 bytes/frame,
\par  for 625/50 is: 829440 bytes/frame.
\par 
\par A FILE: is a contiguous byte stream composed of a sequences of frames as described in section 2.3 above. {\lang1033 These files will thus have a total byte count of
\par for 525/60:  320 frames = 223948800 bytes/sequence,
\par for 625/50:  270 frames = 223948800 bytes/sequence
\par }
\par Multiplex structure:  Cb Y Cr [Y] ...  1440 bytes/line
\par 720  Y's/line
\par 360 Cb's/line
\par 360 Cr's/line
\par 
\par TABLE 2: Format summary
\par \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx1985\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx5367\clbrdrt
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx8749 \pard \qc\sb136\widctlpar\intbl\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \cell -- 525/60 --\cell -- 625/50 --\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl 
\row \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 
\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx1985\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx5367\clbrdrt
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx8749 \pard \qc\sb136\widctlpar\intbl\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 active lines\cell 486\cell 576\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl \row 
\pard \qc\sb136\widctlpar\intbl\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 frame size (bytes)\cell 699840\cell 829440\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl \row \pard \qc\sb136\widctlpar\intbl\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 fields/sec (Hz)\cell 60\cell 50\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl 
\row \pard \qc\sb136\widctlpar\intbl\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 Top-Field  (F1)\cell LATER\cell EARLIER\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl \row \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrb
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx1985
\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx5367\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \clbrdrr
\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrcf1 \cellx8749 \pard \qc\sb136\widctlpar\intbl\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 Seq-length (bytes)\cell {\lang1033 223948800}\cell {\lang1033 223948800}\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl \row \pard\plain 
\s1\fi-432\li432\sb480\widctlpar\tx432\tx794\tx2127\tx2410\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186356}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104077}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269672}3\tab Testing procedures and schedule{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186356}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104077}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269672}
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186357}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104078}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269673}3.1\tab Submission of intent before June 22 1998{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186357}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104078}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269673}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 
The submission procedure is dealt with in separate ITU contributions (e.g., COM 12-30, December 1997). All proponents wishing to propose their objective video quality models for ITU recommendation should submit an intent to participate to the VQEG chair (
see footnote 2, page 3) by June 22{\super nd,} 1998. The submission should include a written description of the model containing principles and available test results in a fashion that does not violate proponents\rquote  intellectual property rights. 

\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186358}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104079}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269674}3.2\tab Final Submission of executable model before August 7{\super th} 1998
{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186358}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104079}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269674}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 
A set of 4 source and processed video sequence pairs will be used as test vectors. They were made available to all proponents, at the beginning of April 1998, in the final file format to be used in the test. 
\par Each proponent will send an executable of the model, together with the test vector outputs, by July 22{\super nd}
 , 1998 to an independent lab(s) selected by the ILSC. The executable version of the model must run correctly on one of the three following computing environments:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}\pard \fi-360\li360\sb136\widctlpar\tx360\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
SUN SPARC workstation running the Solaris 2.3 UNIX operating system (SUN OS 5.5). 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}WINDOWS NT Version 4.0 workstation.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}SGI workstation running IRIX Version no [to be decided]. 
\par \pard \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 Alternately, proponents may supply object code working on either the computers of the independent lab(s) or on a computer provided by the proponent. The proponents have untill August 7{\super th}
 to get their code running.
\par 
\par The independent lab will verify that the software produces the same results as the proponent with a maximum error of 0.1%. If greater errors are found,  the independent lab and proponent lab will work together to discover the sources of errors and correct
 them. If the errors cannot be corrected, then the ILSC will review the result{\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186359}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104080}s and recommend further action.
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269675}3.3\tab Results analysis{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186359}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104080}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269675}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 The results as provided by the proponents and verified by the independent lab(s) will be analyzed to derive the evaluation metrics of section {\b 4}
. These metrics are calculated by each proponent and verified by the ILSC, or they may be calculated completely by the ILSC and verified by the proponents. The results will be reported anonymously to the outside world (proponent a,b,c,..) but identified b
y proponent to VQEG.
\par \pard\plain \s1\fi-432\li432\sb480\widctlpar\tx432\tx794\tx2127\tx2410\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186360}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269676}4\tab {\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104081}Objective quality model evaluation criteria{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186360}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104081}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269676}
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186361}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104082}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269677}4.1\tab Introduction to evaluation metrics{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186361}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104082}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269677}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 
A number of attributes characterize the performance of an objective video quality model as an estimator of video picture quality in a variety of applications. These attributes are listed in the following sections as:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}\pard \fi-360\li1080\sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1080\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}Prediction Accuracy
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}Prediction Monotonicity
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang2057 \'b7\tab}Prediction Consistency
\par \pard \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 This section lists a set of metrics to measure these attributes. The metrics are derived from the objective model outputs and the results 
from viewer subjective rating of the test sequences. Both objective and subjective tests will provide a single number (figure of merit) for each Source and Processed sequence pair that correlates with the video quality difference between the Source and Pr
ocessed sequences. It is presumed that the subjective results include mean ratings and error confidence intervals that take into account differences within the viewer population and differences between multiple subjective testing labs.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186362}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104083}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269678}4.2\tab Prediction nonlinearity{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186362}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104083}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269678}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 The outputs by the objective video quality model (the VQR\rquote s) should be correlated with the viewer DMOS\rquote 
s in a predictable and repeatable fashion. The relationship between predicted VQR and DMOS need not be linear as subjective testing can have nonlinear q
uality rating compression at the extremes of the test range. It is not the linearity of the relationship that is critical, but the stability of the relationship and a data set\rquote 
s error-variance from the relationship that determine predictive usefulne
ss. To remove any nonlinearities due to the subjective rating process (see Figure 1.) and to facilitate comparison of the models in a common analysis space, the relationship between each model\rquote 
s predictions and the subjective ratings will be estimated using a nonlinear regression between the model\rquote s set of VQR\rquote s and the corresponding DMOS\rquote s.
\par {\fs24 
\par }\pard \qc\sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 {\fs20 {\pict\wmetafile8\picw7620\pich5080\picwgoal4320\pichgoal2880 \box\brdrs\brdrw15 \picscalex89\picscaley90 
