00000000000000000000000000000000000000}}{\fs24 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s47\qc\sa720\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 Figure 1.  Example Relationship between VQR and DMOS
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 The nonlinear regression will be fitted to the [VQR,DMOS] data set and be restricted to be monotonic over the range of VQR\rquote 
s. The functional form of the nonlinear regression is not critical except that it be monotonic, reasonably general, and have a minimum number of free parameters to avoid overfitting of the data. As the nature of the nonlinearities are not well 
known beforehand, several functional forms will be regressed for each model and the one with the best fit (in a least squares sense) will be used for that model. 
\par The functional forms to be regressed are listed below. Each regression will be with the constraint that the function is monotonic on the full interval of quality values:
\par \pard\plain \s57\widctlpar \f5\fs22\lang2057 {\b\i\lang1033 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\widctlpar\tx864\tx1191\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\b0 4.2.1.1\tab (1) The  4-parameter cubic polynomial 
\par }\pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057       DMOS{\sub p}(VQR) = A0 + A1*(VQR ) + A2*(VQR)^2+  A3*(VQR)^3 
\par       fitted to the data [VQR,DMOS].
\par (2) The same polynomial form as in (1) applied to the "inverse data" [DMOS, VQR].
\par (3) The 5-parameter logistic curve:
\par         DMOS{\sub p}(VQR) = A0 + (A1-A0)/(1 + ( (X+A5)/A3)^A4 )
\par         fitted to the data [VQR,DMOS].
\par The chosen nonlinear regression function will be used to transform the set of VQR values to a set of  predicted MOS values, DMOS{\sub p}(VQR), which will then be compared with the actual DMOS values from the subjective tests. 
\par Besides carrying out an analysis on the mean one can do the same analysis on the individual Opinion Scores (OS), leading to individual Differential Opinion Scores (DOS). This has the advantage of taking into account the variations between subjects. For ob
jective models there is no variance and thus OS{\sub p}= MOS{\sub p }and DOS{\sub p}=DMOS{\sub p}.
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186363}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104084}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269679}4.3\tab Evaluation metrics{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186363}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104084}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269679}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 This section lists the evaluation metrics to be calculated on the subjective and objective data. Once the nonlinear transformation of section {\b 4.2}
 has been applied, the objective model\rquote s prediction performance is then evaluated by computing various metrics on the actual sets of subjectively measured  DMOS and the predicted DMOS{\sub p}
. The set of differences between measured and predicted DMOS is defined as the quality-error set Qerror[]:
\par \tab Qerror[i] = DMOS[i] \endash  DMOS{\sub p}[i] 
\par where the index \lquote I\rquote  refers to an individual processed video sequence.
\par {\i\ul Metrics relating to Prediction Accuracy of a model}{\ul 
\par }\pard\plain \s57\widctlpar \f5\fs22\lang2057 {\lang1033 
\par }\pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 {\b Metric1:} \tab The  Pearson linear correlation coefficient between DOS{\sub p}  and DOS, including a test of significance of the difference.
\par {\b Metric2:} \tab The  Pearson linear correlation coefficient between DMOS{\sub p}  and DMOS.
\par {\i\ul Metrics relating to Prediction Monotonicity of a model}{\ul 
\par }{\b Metric3}: \tab Spearman rank order correlation coefficient between DMOS{\sub p}  and DMOS.
\par A pair-wise comparison of pairs of HRC\rquote 
s on a scene by scene basis has also been proposed for examining the correlation between subjective preferences and objective preferences, and merits further investigation by the VQEG for inclusion in these tests.
\par {\b 
\par }\pard\plain \s57\widctlpar \f5\fs22\lang2057 {\i\ul Metrics relating to Prediction Consistency of a model}{\ul 
\par }
\par {\b Metric4}: \tab Outlier Ratio of  \ldblquote outlier-points\rdblquote  to total points N. 
\par 
\par \tab \tab  Outlier Ratio = (total number of outliers)/N
\par \tab \tab 
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 where an outlier is a point for which:  ABS[ Qerror[i] ] > 2*DMOSStandardError[i]. 
\par \pard\plain \s57\widctlpar \f5\fs22\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 Twice the DMOS Standard Error is used as the threshold for defining an outlier point. 
\par \pard\plain \s57\widctlpar \f5\fs22\lang2057 
\par {\lang1033 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186364}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104085}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269680}4.4\tab Generalizability{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186364}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104085}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269680}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 
Generalizability is the ability of a model to perform reliably over a very broad set of video content. This is obviously a critical selection factor given the very wide variety of content found in real applications. There is no specific metric that is spe
cific to generalizability so this objective testing procedure requires the selection of as broad a set of representative test sequences as is possible. The test sequences and specific HRC\rquote s will be selected by the experts of the VQEG\rquote 
s Independent Labs and Selection Committee (ILSC) and should ensure broad coverage of typical content (spatial detail, motion complexity, color, etc.) and typical video 
processing conditions. The breadth of the test set will determine how well the generalizability of the models is tested. At least 20 different scenes are recommended as a minimum set of test sequences. It is suggested that some quantitative measures (e.g.
, criticality, spatial and temporal energy) are used in the selection of the test sequences to verify the diversity of the test set.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\fi-576\li576\sb313\widctlpar\tx576\tx794\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186365}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104086}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269681}4.5\tab Complexity{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186365}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104086}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269681}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 {\lang1033 
The performance of a model as measured by the above Metrics #1-7 will be used as the primary basis for model recommendation. If several models are similar in performance, then the VQEG may choose to take model complexity into account in formulating their 
recommendations if the intended application has a requirement for minimum complexity. The VQEG will define the complexity criteria if and when required. 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\fi-432\li432\sb480\widctlpar\tx432\tx794\tx2127\tx2410\tx2921\tx3261 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186366}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc421104087}
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc423269682}5\tab Recommendation decision{\*\bkmkend _Toc411186366}{\*\bkmkend _Toc421104087}{\*\bkmkend _Toc423269682}
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 
The VQEG will recommend methods of objective video quality assessment based on the primary evaluation metrics defined in section 4.3 The final decision(s) on ITU Recommendations will be made by the Study Groups involved: ITU-T SG 12, ITU-T SG\~
9, and ITU-R SG\~11.
\par It is expected that an important measure of model acceptability, and the strength of the recommendation, will be the relative comparison of model rating errors compared to rating
 errors between different groups of subjective viewers. The selection procedure will require subjective rating cross-correlation data from the DSCQS experiments to estimate individual and population rating variances. This may require both duplication of s
equences across different subjective testing labs and duplication of sequences within any one  subjective test experiment. 
\par If  the metrics of section 4.3 are insufficient for developing a recommendation, then model complexity may be used as a further crite
rion for evaluation. The preference is one recommended model, but multiple models are possible. If the VQEG judges that a significantly improved recommended model can be developed from some combination of the proposed objective quality models, then this a
ctivity falls outside the scope of this plan and the VQEG may charter a follow-on task to address this activity.{\b\kerning28 
\par }\pard\plain \s48\qc\sb720\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 \page Annex 1
\par \pard\plain \s50\qc\sb240\sa284\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 Objective Video Quality Model Attributes
\par \pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 Section {\b 4} presents several important attributes, and supporting metrics, that relate to an objective quality model\rquote s ability to predict a viewer\rquote 
s rating of the difference between two video sequences. This annex provides further background on the nature of these a
ttributes, and serves as a guide to the selection of metrics appropriate for measuring each attribute. The discussion is in terms of the relation between the subjective DMOS data and the model\rquote s transformed DMOS{\sub p}
 data. The schematic data and lines are not real, but idealized examples only meant to illustrate the discussion. In the interest of clarity, only a few points are used to illustrate the relationship between objective DMOS{\sub p}
 and subjective DMOS, and error bars on the subjective DMOS data are left out. 
\par {\i\fs24\ul Attribute1: Prediction Accuracy
\par }This attribute is simply the ability of the model to predict the viewers\rquote  DMOS ratings with a minimum error \ldblquote on average\rdblquote . The model in Figure 2 is seen to have a lower average error between DMOS{\sub p}
 and DMOS than the model in Figure 3, and has therefore greater prediction accuracy.
\par {\fs24 
\par }\pard \qc\sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 {\fs20 {\pict\wmetafile8\picw7620\pich5080\picwgoal4320\pichgoal2880 \box\brdrs\brdrw15 \picscalex85 
