\par }\pard\plain \s47\qc\sa720\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \b\f5\fs22\lang2057 {\lang1033    Fig. 6.  Model with large outlying errors  \tab       Fig. 7. Model with consistent errors
\par }\pard\plain \sb136\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 
Figures 6 and 7 show models with approximately equal RMS errors between predicted and measured DMOS. Figure 6 is an example of a model that has quite accurate predictions for the majority of sequences but has large prediction error for the two points in t
he middle of the figure. Figure 7 is an example of  a model that has a balanced set of prediction errors - it is not as accurate as the model of Figure 6 for most of the sequences but it performs \ldblquote consistently\rdblquote 
 by providing reasonable predictions for all the sequences. The model\rquote 
s prediction consistency can be measured by the number of outlier points (defined as having an error greater than a given threshold such as one confidence interval) as a fraction of the total number of points. A smaller outlier fraction means the model
\rquote s predictions are more consistent. Another metric that relates to consistency is Kurtosis, which is a dimensionless quantity that relates only to the shape of the error distribution and not to the distribution\rquote 
s width. Two models may have identical RMS error, but the model with an error distribution having larger \ldblquote tails\rdblquote  to the distribution will have a greater Kurtosis.
\par {\fs24 
\par }\pard\plain \s48\qc\sb720\widctlpar\tx794\tx1191\tx1588\tx1985 \f5\fs22\lang2057 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc411186367}Annex 2
